WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.792
A payment service keeps each payment in PostgreSQL.

2
00:00:05.792 --> 00:00:09.732
The support team searches a second copy of it, in a search index.

3
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:17.193
The service keeps that copy fresh the obvious way: update the database, then send the same change to the index.

4
00:00:17.193 --> 00:00:18.501
That's a dual write.

5
00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:22.853
Two requests for payment pay-981 arrive a millisecond apart.

6
00:00:22.853 --> 00:00:27.597
A capture job sets it to captured, and a refund sets it to refunded.

7
00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:30.255
The capture commits to the database first.

8
00:00:30.255 --> 00:00:35.055
Then its thread stalls for a few milliseconds, say in a garbage collection pause.

9
00:00:35.233 --> 00:00:38.863
The refund commits next, so the database says refunded.

10
00:00:38.863 --> 00:00:41.305
That's right: the refund came second.

11
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.157
The refund also updates the index, which now says refunded too.

12
00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:49.516
Then the capture wakes up and makes its second write.

13
00:00:49.516 --> 00:00:51.180
The index now says captured.

14
00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:53.346
Every call reported success.

15
00:00:53.346 --> 00:01:00.063
There's no error and no log line, and the index stays wrong until someone writes pay-981 again.

16
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:05.521
The cause: two writers, each picking its own order, and neither store follows the other.

17
00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:09.007
A database's own replicas never have this problem.

18
00:01:09.007 --> 00:01:15.816
One leader picks the order of writes, records it in a log, and every copy applies the log in that order.

19
00:01:15.967 --> 00:01:17.381
So do the same here.

20
00:01:17.381 --> 00:01:20.069
The services write only to PostgreSQL.

21
00:01:20.069 --> 00:01:22.685
The index follows the database's log.

22
00:01:22.867 --> 00:01:24.528
Run the race again.

23
00:01:24.528 --> 00:01:28.637
The capture locks the row, so the refund waits.

24
00:01:28.637 --> 00:01:34.407
The log records the capture as commit 768, then the refund as 769.

25
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:40.890
The follower applies the log in order, captured then refunded, and remembers how far it got.

26
00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:42.815
Both stores end at refunded.

27
00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:47.221
The price is lag: the index trails the database by a few milliseconds.

28
00:01:47.221 --> 00:01:51.409
But it can't disagree about the order, because it no longer picks one.
